      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! When it comes to delay metrics, such as the average control delay per vehicle, there's quite a bit of variability depending on the specific design and traffic conditions of each intersection or roundabout.\n\nFor signalized intersections, the average control delay per vehicle can vary widely. In general, during peak urban hours, the average control delay can range from 10 to 30 seconds per vehicle. This means that each vehicle may experience an average delay of 10 to 30 seconds while waiting for a green light. The higher the saturation level, the longer the delay, as more vehicles are competing for limited road space.\n\nIn contrast, modern roundabouts typically have lower average control delays. The continuous flow of traffic allows vehicles to move more smoothly, reducing the time spent waiting at intersections. The average control delay per vehicle in a roundabout can range from 2 to 10 seconds, depending on the specific design and traffic conditions. This means that each vehicle may experience an average delay of 2 to 10 seconds while navigating the roundabout.\n\nIt's important to note that these are just typical ranges, and the actual delay metrics can vary depending on a variety of factors, such as the specific design of the intersection or roundabout, the volume of traffic,",
